 Bare           names at both ends: a stored pathname loses its leading slash.  arc      the same, both builds: `Cannot move A000003.arc to <target>'.           It works if the archive name is RELATIVE.  todos    converts into `todos.$$$.<n>' IN THE DATA DIRECTORY and then           forks OS-9's own `del' and `rename' to move it over the           original, which is where it stops.  The file you named is           untouched; the DOS text is in the temporary.  It turns every           CR into CR LF and appends one stray LF.  toos9    the same the other way round -- CR LF back to CR, in           `toos9.$$$.<n>' -- and it appends one 0xFF byte.  Each writes           its end-of-file marker into the file and they do not agree on           what it is.  autolf   given a FILENAME it converts through a temporary and reports           `couldn't rename'.  As a FILTER -- `autolf -c -C -L < in > out'           -- it never renames anything and works perfectly.  newslock the other half of the same gap, and the only one where the           missing call IS the program: `newslock <tempname> <lockname>'           makes a news lock by LINKING one name to the other, which is           how a lock is made atomic on a Unix filesystem.  No link(),           so it returns 1 and leaves nothing behind.           SRC/cnews/misc/newslock.c is four lines.  Measured 2026-09-19.AUTOLF IS THE ANSWER TO todos AND toos9.  It is Mike Tozer's converter,1995: CR to CRLF or LF and back, tabs expanded, ^Z handled, `-H' explainsthe conversions.  40 bytes of OS-9 text in, 41 out, the trailing 0D now0D 0A.  Nothing on this disk needed writing for that job; it was alreadyhere under a name DOC/INDEX described as an `auto-linefeed filter'.--------------------------------------------------------------TWO PROGRAMS THAT ONLY WORK TOGETHER -- 2026-08-29--------------------------------------------------------------`wisecrack' and `ask' were both scored MUTE by every stage of every s