------------------------------------------------------------Neither set is broken.  cjpeg and djpeg separate the fields of aPNM header with LF; the netpbm ports on this disk use CR, whichis what an OS-9 C compiler writes for `\n'.  Neither will readthe other's file, and each blames the data:    cjpeg:    Bogus data in PPM file    ppmtopgm: junk in file where an integer should beThree bytes are the whole of the difference.  A P6 or P5 headeris magic, separator, dimensions, separator, maxval, separator,and `pbyte' patches each one in place:    ppmmake red 8 8 > red.ppm       netpbm writes CR    pbyte red.ppm 2 0a    pbyte red.ppm 6 0a              `8 8' is three characters    pbyte red.ppm a 0a    cjpeg red.ppm > red.jpg         and now cjpeg reads it    djpeg -pnm red.jpg > back.ppm   djpeg writes LF    pbyte back.ppm 2 0d    pbyte back.ppm 6 0d    pbyte back.ppm a 0d    pnmfile back.ppm                PPM raw, 8 by 8The offsets move with the number of digits in the dimensions --count them.  For the library's own 227x149 test image they are2, A and E.DOC/STATUS said until today that cjpeg `refuses every PNM on thisdisk' and that djpeg had nothing to decompress.  Both were trueobservations and both conclusions were wrong: the JPEG on thedisk was corrupt (see above) and the PNMs were fine but writtenin the other convention.  Seven cases in tools/datatests/netpbm.cases now hold all of this down, including a full roundtrip -- netpbm makes an image, cjpeg compresses it, djpeg bringsit back, and netpbm reads the result.--------------------------------------------------------------THREE THAT DID NOT COME BACK -- 2026-08-29, ALL THREE ANSWERED BY 2026-09-19--------------------------------------------------------------A program that hangs is worse than one that fails, because a sweep scores itSILENT and moves on while a person waits.  Three were found by giving themreal work rather than `-?'.  NOT ONE OF THE THREE WAS THE PROGRAM'S FAULT,which is the lesson of this section: one was waiting for a person to type,one had been fixed weeks before and the note outlived the fix, and one wasthe emulator freezing.  Each is kept below with what it used to do, becausethe shape of the mistake is worth more than the entry:  names   BOTH HALVES OF THIS ENTRY WERE WRONG, corrected 2026-09-19.          `names' takes no module and lists nothing: it is a German          ADDRESS BOOK, `Adressen Verwaltung' 1.0, full screen, which          creates a file of its own under SYS on first run and then          asks `Geben Sie einen Buchstaben ein'.  Fed no keyboard it          re-prompts for ever, which is what the hang was.  Run at a          terminal it is an address book and behaves like one.          DOC/INDEX had already been corrected; this had not.  nroff   FIXED 2026-08-31 and this note outlived it.  nroff was one of          the eleven cio casualties rebuilt trap-free that day (85ef9377)          and it works: it formats a file bare and under a login session,          and `man' on this disk runs `nroff -man' for every manual page          it shows.  Re-measured 2026-09-19.  `roff' and `proff' work too.  yacc    FIXED IN THE EMULATOR 2026-09-19 (os9exec 8ee3bcf), the same          masked-signal freeze as pgmedge.  It reads a grammar, reports          what it finds wrong with it, and writes y.tab.c.  `bison' is          the other one here and was never affected.  Was: printed          nothing, wrote no files and never returned.None of the three is run by a capture or a test.  That was because a hangwedges the session and everything after it reports nothing; now that noneof them hangs, `nroff' and `yacc' could each carry one, and `yacc' needs abuild of the emulator at or past 8ee3bcf for it to come out right.--------------------------------------------------------------KNOWN GOOD, WITH A CONDITION--------------------------------------------------------------  advent      reads 